# November 19, 2026: a first scatter of places across Colorado (region co), claimed by tools/choose.py --thinnest because
# Colorado had one place on the map (Animas Forks). Seventeen places spread over the state: mining ghost towns in the
# mountains (Saint Elmo, Independence), the Redstone coke ovens and the Crystal Mill in the Crystal River valley, Leadville's
# Tabor Opera House, Georgetown's Hotel de Paris, the South Park City museum town at Fairplay, the Santa Fe Trail country of
# the Arkansas valley (Bent's Old Fort, Boggsville), Fort Garland in the San Luis Valley, the Ludlow tent colony site, the
# Black homesteading town of Dearfield on the plains, the Rainbow Arch Bridge at Fort Morgan, Lowry Pueblo in Canyons of the
# Ancients, and in the far north-west the Bromide charcoal kilns and the Two-Bar Ranch in Browns Park. Sources were opened in
# this session: Wikipedia as raw text (tools/wiki_raw.py); National Register forms from NPGallery (Rainbow Arch Bridge
# 85000221, Bromide Charcoal Kilns 00000740, Two-Bar Ranch 78000873, Convicts' Bread Oven 74000589; text layer read); and
# through tools/page.py the managers' pages (History Colorado, the National Park Service, the BLM, the Fish and Wildlife
# Service, Pitkin County, the Aspen Historical Society, the Bent County historical society, the South Park Historical
# Foundation), HMdb and a few news pages named in the links.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'co_qmtv','label':'Colorado: a first scatter','added':'2026-11-19 21:24'}
PLACES=[
N('saint-elmo-co','Saint Elmo ghost town','Nathrop','co','town','standing',38.70472,-106.345,'area','view','drive','','1880–1922',
  'Saint Elmo, first called Forest City, was founded in 1880 in the Sawatch Range when gold and silver mining began, and nearly 2,000 people settled there. At its 1890s peak it had a telegraph office, a general store, a town hall, five hotels, saloons, dance halls, a newspaper and a school. Most miners worked the Mary Murphy, Teresa C., Molly and Pioneer mines; the Mary Murphy kept going until the Denver, South Park and Pacific railroad was abandoned in 1922. The post office closed in 1952, and the town is listed on the National Register as the Saint Elmo Historic District.',
  'Wikipedia calls it one of the best preserved ghost towns in Colorado: many buildings stand along Main Street, though the town hall and a few others burned in 2002 and Buena Vista Heritage rebuilt the town hall in 2008. The general store opens in summer. Interiors not checked.',
  'A whole 1880s mining street still standing at nearly 10,000 feet.',
  'Saint Elmo is still lived in, and Wikipedia says the residents ask visitors to treat the town with respect: look from the street. Reached by Chaffee County Road 162 up Chalk Creek, about 20 miles south-west of Buena Vista. OpenStreetMap maps a parking lot on CR 162 about 500 m east of the town centre, on San Isabel National Forest land. The summer hours of the general store were not checked.',
  [W('St. Elmo, Colorado')],th=[38.70548,-106.33929],thn='Lot on CR 162 east of town (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?'),
N('independence-ghost-town-co','Independence ghost town','Aspen','co','town','ruins',39.10651,-106.60545,'site','public','walk','','1879–1912',
  'Gold was struck near Independence Pass around July 4, 1879, and a tent camp became a mining town of about 1,000 people by 1882, with the Farwell Gold Mill and some 40 businesses. The shallow gold ran out quickly and the mines and mill closed in August 1882; Aspen\'s silver drew people away. In the winter of 1899 record snow cut the town off, and the last residents made skis from planks and left for Aspen. It has been a ghost town since at least 1912 and was listed on the National Register in 1973.',
  'Wikipedia, from the Register form: 26 buildings remain in some form, eight of them intact, all log cabins, some without roofs, in two groups (the townsite and the mill site, about 0.3 mile apart), with foundations. The Aspen Historical Society has restored cabins and laid out paths and interpretive signs.',
  'The town that skied out en masse in the winter of 1899.',
  'In White River National Forest; the Aspen Historical Society runs the site under a Forest Service permit. Its page: 16 miles east of Aspen on Highway 82 just below Independence Pass, self-guided, $5 suggested donation in an honor box at the trail entrance, no dogs (read November 2026). Wikipedia says part of the old townsite near the river is private land and that Highway 82 is closed from late October to mid-May. OpenStreetMap maps a pull-off lot on CO 82 about 200 m east of the pin.',
  [W('Independence, Pitkin County, Colorado'),L('Aspen Historical Society: Independence Ghost Town','https://aspenhistory.org/independence-ghost-town/')],th=[39.10681,-106.6035],thn='Pull-off on CO 82 (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
N('redstone-coke-ovens','Redstone coke ovens','Redstone','co','industry','ruins',39.18137,-107.24197,'site','public','drive','','1899–1909',
  'John Cleveland Osgood\'s Colorado Fuel and Iron built 249 beehive ovens at Redstone by the end of 1899 to turn the valley\'s coal into coke, the largest coking works in the state at the time. Mining and coking ended by 1909. The steel was taken for scrap in the Second World War, and the ovens were later lived in by newcomers to Redstone. Pitkin County took over the land in the mid-2000s after a plan to clear some ovens for a gas station, and restoration work began in 2011.',
  'About 90 ovens remain in a 600-foot arc on the west side of Highway 133, most of them stone domes covered in hardened earth; four have been restored to their original look. A middle group stands behind a later stone retaining wall. Small parking area and interpretive plaque.',
  'Beehive ovens from the coke boom that built Redstone.',
  'Pitkin County says the ovens are part of its Coke Ovens Open Space (page read November 2026). Wikipedia\'s description, from older sources, says the property is owned by Mid-Continent Coal and Coke, whose gate is in the middle of the row; that is not confirmed and the county page is newer. Park in the small lot on CO 133 at the south entrance to Redstone.',
  [W('Redstone Coke Oven Historic District'),L('Pitkin County: Redstone Parks and Open Space','https://pitkincounty.com/1113/Redstone-Parks-and-Open-Space')],conf='mixed',th=[39.18106,-107.24203],thn='Coke ovens lot, CO 133',brick='?'),
N('crystal-mill-co','Crystal Mill (Sheep Mountain Power House)','Marble','co','industry','standing',39.05889,-107.10389,'area','view','hike','','1892–1917',
  'The wooden mill on a rock above the Crystal River was built in 1892 or 1893 (Wikipedia gives both) by George C. Eaton and B.S. Phillips as the power plant for the Sheep Mountain Tunnel. It is not a grist mill but a compressor station: a horizontal water wheel drove an air compressor for the silver mines nearby, and a three-stamp ore mill stood next to it. It fell out of use when the mine closed in 1917 and was listed on the National Register in 1985.',
  'The timber power house stands on its outcrop over the river at the old town of Crystal. Interior not checked.',
  'The most photographed old mill in Colorado is a compressed-air plant.',
  'The mill is private property. The Colorado Mountain Club route page (read November 2026) describes about 8 miles round trip on a rough 4WD road up the Crystal River from Marble, says getting down to the riverside for a close view usually means paying the owner a small fee and signing a waiver, that a Forest Service parking permit is needed, and that the road is in avalanche terrain. Those terms are from the club page, not the owner\'s; check before going.',
  [W('Crystal Mill'),L('Colorado Mountain Club: Crystal Mill','https://www.cmc.org/education-adventure/trips/routes-places/crystal-mill')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('tabor-opera-house','Tabor Opera House','Leadville','co','civic','standing',39.24674,-106.29074,'exact','open','drive','','1879',
  'Horace Tabor built the opera house in Leadville in 1879, said by its own site to have taken 100 days; it opened on November 20, 1879, and was the costliest building in Colorado at the time, with materials hauled from Denver by wagon. Tabor lost it after the silver crash of 1893. The Elks bought it in 1901 and reopened it as the Elks Opera House in 1902, and later owners opened it for tours and shows. The National Trust has named it a national treasure.',
  'Three storeys of stone, brick and iron, once called Leadville\'s finest brick structure. Inside, Wikipedia counts fourteen collections of scenery and stage machinery from 1879 to 1902, and the opera house\'s own site mentions original red velvet seats. A museum of memorabilia.',
  'Stage scenery from 1879 still hanging in a silver-boom opera house.',
  'Run as a cultural centre with tours and performances (taboroperahouse.net). The Leadville and Twin Lakes tourism page says group guided historic tours run Friday to Sunday from Memorial Day weekend to the end of September, and private tours by appointment; that is not the opera house\'s own page, and times were not checked.',
  [W('Tabor Opera House'),L('Tabor Opera House','https://taboroperahouse.net/'),L('Leadville Twin Lakes: Tabor Opera House','https://www.leadvilletwinlakes.com/listing/tabor-opera-house/')],brick='some',hours='?'),
N('hotel-de-paris-georgetown','Hotel de Paris Museum','Georgetown','co','hotel','standing',39.70586,-105.6958,'exact','open','drive','','1875',
  'Louis Dupuy, a French immigrant from Alençon, opened the Hotel de Paris on 6th Street in 1875 at the height of Georgetown\'s silver boom, and it became known for luxury and French cooking. He enlarged it by 1881 with more rooms and a laundry, and built a commercial kitchen. The Colorado Dames (National Society of the Colonial Dames of America) bought it in 1954 and have run it as a museum since.',
  'Wikipedia: the interiors hold over 5,000 Victorian items, about 90 per cent of them from Dupuy\'s time, and the kitchen and dining room survive. Guided tours.',
  'A French chef\'s mining-town hotel with nearly all its furniture.',
  'Museum on 6th Street across from Georgetown Town Hall, run by the Colonial Dames. Its How to Visit page could not be read in this session (connection failed): hours and admission not checked.',
  [W('Hotel de Paris (Georgetown, Colorado)'),L('Hotel de Paris Museum: How to Visit','https://hoteldeparismuseum.org/how-to-visit/')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('south-park-city','South Park City museum town','Fairplay','co','town','standing',39.22528,-106.00444,'site','open','walk','','1860s–1900',
  'South Park City is an open-air museum at the west end of Front Street in Fairplay, opened in 1959 by the South Park Historical Foundation. It gathers about 35 old buildings, most moved in from abandoned camps and ghost towns of South Park, into a mining town of 1860 to 1900, with over 60,000 artifacts. The South Park Brewery and the Summer Saloon are on the National Register, and Father Dyer\'s Chapel was a log hotel in Montgomery rebuilt here as a church in 1868.',
  'Period rooms and shops in the moved buildings (the foundation\'s page). Living History Days on the second weekend of August, per Wikipedia.',
  'A ghost town assembled from other ghost towns.',
  'Run by the South Park Historical Foundation; tickets at the visitor center. Its page (read November 2026): adults $12, children 6 to 12 $6, under 6 free; no food, drink or smoking in the museum.',
  [W('South Park City'),L('South Park City Museum','https://southparkcity.org')],brick='?',hours='May 15 to Oct 15: 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.; Friday of Memorial Day weekend to Labor Day 9 a.m. to 5:30 p.m. (ticket sales)'),
N('fort-garland','Fort Garland','Fort Garland','co','fort','standing',37.42397,-105.43224,'exact','open','drive','forts','1858–1883',
  'Fort Garland was built in 1858 to house two companies of soldiers guarding settlers in the San Luis Valley, then in New Mexico Territory, and was named for General John Garland. Kit Carson commanded New Mexico Volunteers here after the Civil War and made a treaty with the Utes in 1867. Buffalo Soldiers of the Ninth Cavalry were posted here from 1876 to 1879. The Army left in 1883; the Colorado Historical Society restored the remaining buildings and opened a museum in 1950.',
  'Adobe buildings around the parade ground, restored and partly reconstructed: the commandant\'s quarters recreated as in Carson\'s time, the cavalry barracks and officers\' quarters, with exhibits on Carson, the Buffalo Soldiers and Hispano arts. Paths between buildings are dirt.',
  'Kit Carson\'s adobe post in the San Luis Valley.',
  'Fort Garland Museum and Cultural Center, run by History Colorado. Its page (read November 2026): free on-site parking; phone 719-379-3512 for guided tours. The San Luis Valley Museum Association page gives admission as $7 adults and $5 seniors.',
  [W('Fort Garland'),L('History Colorado: Fort Garland Museum','https://historycolorado.org/node/3551'),L('Museum Trail: Fort Garland','https://www.museumtrail.org/fort-garland-museum')],brick='no',hours='Mar 1 to Oct 31 daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Nov 1 to Feb 28 daily 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; closed Thanksgiving and Christmas'),
N('bents-old-fort','Bent\'s Old Fort','La Junta','co','fort','standing',38.0406,-103.4294,'site','open','walk','forts','1833–1849',
  'Charles and William Bent and Ceran St. Vrain built an adobe trading fort here on the Arkansas in 1833 to trade with the Southern Cheyenne and Arapaho for buffalo robes. For most of its 16 years it was the one large American post on the Santa Fe Trail between Missouri and the Mexican settlements, on what was Mexican territory until 1848. William Bent left it in 1849. The site became a National Historic Site in 1960, and the fort was rebuilt in 1976.',
  'What stands is the 1976 adobe reconstruction, built on the excavated footprint from paintings, sketches and diaries, with, Wikipedia says, a good deal of guesswork. The National Park Service says entry is limited to the central plaza on scheduled days, and guided tours go into the furnished rooms.',
  'The Santa Fe Trail\'s great adobe trading post, rebuilt on its own footings.',
  'National Park Service, 35110 State Highway 194, La Junta. Its hours page (updated October 17, 2026) says the park is open and the fort is open for plaza access on a scheduled basis, with days on the park\'s event calendar; no clock times were given. Phone 719-383-5010.',
  [W('Bent\'s Old Fort National Historic Site'),L('NPS: Operating Hours & Seasons','https://www.nps.gov/beol/planyourvisit/hours.htm')],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('boggsville-co','Boggsville','Las Animas','co','town','standing',38.03815,-103.20773,'area','open','walk','','1866–1873',
  'Thomas Boggs built an adobe house here on the Purgatoire River in 1866, and John Wesley Prowers a two-storey 14-room house the next year that served as home, school and stage station on the Purgatory branch of the Santa Fe Trail. Kit Carson spent his last months at Boggsville before dying at Fort Lyon in 1868. It was the seat of Bent County from 1870 to 1872 and faded when the railroads passed it by. The Pioneer Historical Society of Bent County holds 39 acres with the two houses.',
  'The restored Boggs and Prowers houses are the only historic buildings left; Wikipedia calls them among the earliest Territorial-style buildings in Colorado. Self-guided trails and signs on the grounds.',
  'Kit Carson\'s last home town on the Santa Fe Trail.',
  'Boggsville Historic Site, 28120 Colorado Highway 101, about two miles south of Las Animas. The society\'s page (read November 2026): grounds and trails open all year, admission free, guided tours out of season by arrangement; camping on site.',
  [W('Boggsville, Colorado'),L('Bent County Heritage: Boggsville','https://www.bentcountyheritage.org/boggsville/')],brick='?',hours='Grounds all year; house tours last Friday of April to mid-October, Tuesday to Saturday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('ludlow-tent-colony','Ludlow tent colony site and memorial','Trinidad','co','landmark','traces',37.33922,-104.58392,'site','public','walk','','1913–1914',
  'Striking coal miners and their families, about 1,200 people, lived in a tent colony here on land the United Mine Workers had leased. On April 20, 1914, Colorado National Guard soldiers and Colorado Fuel and Iron guards attacked and burned the camp; about 21 people died, most of them women and children, among them two women and eleven children in a cellar. The killing set off ten days of fighting across the coalfields. The union put up a granite monument in 1918.',
  'The 1918 monument (vandalised in 2003 and repaired in 2005), the door over the cellar where the two women and eleven children died, and a row of interpretive signs on the tent colony and its archaeology. Wikipedia calls it one of the best preserved remains of such an encampment.',
  'The cellar where the Ludlow Massacre\'s children died.',
  'The site is owned by the United Mine Workers of America and set out for visitors with signs. HMdb places it near the corner of Las Animas County Roads 44 and 61.5, about a mile west of I-25 exit 27. No parking lot is mapped; hours not checked.',
  [W('Ludlow Massacre'),L('HMdb: Ludlow Tent Colony Site Memorial','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=100506')],brick='?'),
N('dearfield-co','Dearfield','Wiggins','co','town','ruins',40.2906,-104.2594,'area','view','drive','','1910–1940s',
  'Oliver Toussaint Jackson, a Boulder businessman, filed on the homestead in 1910 and advertised for colonists to found a farming town for African American homesteaders. By 1920 Dearfield had 200 to 300 people, two churches, a school and a restaurant. The Depression and Dust Bowl emptied it; by 1940 twelve people were left, and Jackson died in 1948. It was listed on the National Register in 1995, and the National Park Service is studying whether it should become a park unit.',
  'The Park Service study page says two original buildings remain, a filling station and Jackson\'s house, with the line of the main street, building ruins and archaeological remains; Wikipedia also names a diner. Seen from the highway.',
  'One of the last standing towns built by Black homesteaders on the Great Plains.',
  'On Colorado Highway 34, eleven miles west of Wiggins and about 25 miles east of Greeley (Park Service). The Black American West Museum owns parts of the townsite and the rest was in private hands (Wyoming Public Media, 2020): look from the road. No parking lot is mapped.',
  [W('Dearfield, Colorado'),L('NPS: Dearfield Special Resource Study','https://parkplanning.nps.gov/Dearfield_SRS'),L('Wyoming Public Media, 2020','https://www.wyomingpublicmedia.org/2020-06-30/museum-acquires-property-to-protect-black-homesteading-town-in-colorado')],brick='?'),
N('rainbow-arch-bridge-fort-morgan','Rainbow Arch Bridge','Fort Morgan','co','landmark','standing',40.26917,-103.8012,'exact','public','walk','','1922–1988',
  'Morgan County and the state highway department shared the cost of this bridge over the South Platte, built in 1922 and 1923 by Charles Sheely\'s Colorado Bridge and Construction Company to a design by the Marsh Engineering Company of Des Moines. It has eleven 90-foot reinforced concrete rainbow arches and is over 1,100 feet long; when finished it was said to be the longest Marsh arch bridge in the world, and it is the only one in Colorado. A second bridge was built beside it in 1963, and it carried traffic until 1988.',
  'Eleven concrete arches over the river, with the deck hung from them; the HMdb marker is on Highway 52 just north of I-76.',
  'The longest rainbow arch bridge in the world, when it was built.',
  'Closed to cars since 1988 (HMdb marker); the 1985 inventory form gives the owner as the Colorado Department of Highways. OpenStreetMap maps a lot named for the bridge in Riverside Park, on public land, about 175 m east of the pin. Whether walkers may cross it was not checked.',
  [L('HMdb: Rainbow Arch Bridge','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=47194'),L('Register inventory form (NPGallery)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/85000221_text')],th=[40.2668,-103.79937],thn='Rainbow Arch Bridge lot, Riverside Park (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
N('convicts-bread-oven','Convicts\' Bread Oven','Molina','co','landmark','standing',39.200556,-108.191389,'area','view','drive','','1911–1912',
  'A concrete oven shaped like a Mexican adobe oven, built in 1911 to bake bread for a camp of 30 state prison convicts and their guards who were building the road through Plateau Canyon. Bread was baked daily from April 1911 to July 1912. It was listed on the National Register in 1974.',
  'A low concrete box about 7 feet long and 4 feet high with a curved roof, an arched fire opening and a chimney hole; the door is gone (1974 form). It sits halfway between Plateau Creek and Highway 65, about 7 miles east of I-70.',
  'A prison road gang\'s bread oven by the highway.',
  'The 1974 form gives the owner as a private ranch. Whether it can be seen from Highway 65, and where to stop, was not checked: look from the road only.',
  [W('Convicts\' Bread Oven'),L('Register form (NPGallery)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/74000589_text')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('lowry-pueblo','Lowry Pueblo','Pleasant View','co','landmark','ruins',37.58469,-108.92014,'exact','public','walk','','about 1060–1225',
  'An Ancestral Puebloan village begun around 1060 on top of older pithouses and lived in by 40 to 100 people at a time for about 165 years; its last occupation was in the early 1200s. It is unusual for its great kiva, which suggests it was a meeting place for scattered farms before the village grew. Paul Sidney Martin of the Field Museum dug it from 1930 to 1934; the BLM and the University of Colorado stabilised it in the 1960s. It is a National Historic Landmark and part of Canyons of the Ancients National Monument.',
  'The BLM describes a 40-room village: a quarter-mile trail leads to the great house, with a double kiva inside it, and to the great kiva, with signs along the way.',
  'A thousand-year-old great house and great kiva on the open sage plain.',
  'Bureau of Land Management, Canyons of the Ancients National Monument; day use. From Cortez about 18 miles north on US 491 to the Pleasant View and Lowry sign, then west on Road CC following the signs (BLM page read November 2026). Gravel lot for about a dozen cars, vault toilet, picnic tables. Phone 970-882-5600.',
  [W('Lowry Pueblo'),L('BLM: Lowry Pueblo National Historic Landmark','https://www.blm.gov/visit/lowry-pueblo-national-historic-landmark')],th=[37.58509,-108.91926],thn='Lowry Pueblo lot (BLM)',brick='no'),
N('bromide-charcoal-kilns','Bromide charcoal kilns','Maybell','co','industry','ruins',40.5776,-108.657,'area','view','drive','','1898',
  'The Bromide Mining and Milling Company built a smelter with a 15-ton copper blast furnace in the Douglas Mountain district, because hauling ore 90 miles to the railroad at Rock Springs cost too much. The four kilns were built in 1898 to make charcoal for the furnace; the district shipped much copper ore through the First World War. The other buildings in a photograph of about 1903 are gone, though some foundations remain.',
  'Four sandstone beehive kilns about 20 feet across and 20 feet high, in a row backed against a low rise; History Colorado calls them the best surviving kilns of their type in the state.',
  'Four stone beehives in the empty north-west corner of Colorado.',
  'The 2000 Register form says the five-acre site was deeded in 1998 to a local non-profit, Preserving the Last Frontier, with wire fencing and a turnstile gate round the kilns. Current access, and whether they can be seen from County Road 10, was not checked.',
  [L('Register form (NPGallery)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/00000740_text'),L('History Colorado: Bromide Charcoal Kilns','https://historycolorado.org/node/6741')],brick='no'),
N('two-bar-ranch-browns-park','Two-Bar Ranch headquarters','Maybell','co','landmark','ruins',40.7861,-108.8944,'area','public','walk','','1880s–1920s',
  'The ruined headquarters of the Two-Bar, which the Register list calls one of the largest cattle ranches in Colorado and Wyoming, established in 1887. The 1978 form says some cabins may have been built by Thomas White in the late 1880s and enlarged by the Hoy family after 1895, and that the place went to pieces after a sheep outfit took it over. Some logs were said to have come from Fort Davy Crockett.',
  'The 1978 form lists a hewn-log ranch house, a bunkhouse of 1920, two log dugouts, three other log buildings including a blacksmith shop, a privy and pole corrals, all badly deteriorated, roofs fallen in on several. What stands now was not checked.',
  'A cattle empire\'s headquarters gone to greasewood in Browns Park.',
  'In Browns Park National Wildlife Refuge (Fish and Wildlife Service), about a mile south of Highway 318 per the form. The refuge page (read November 2026): open sunrise to sunset all year, longer in hunting seasons; overnight parking only in the campgrounds; travel off the roads on foot. Where to park for the ranch was not checked.',
  [L('Register form (NPGallery)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/78000873_text'),L('Browns Park National Wildlife Refuge','https://www.fws.gov/refuge/browns-park')],brick='no',hours='Sunrise to sunset all year (refuge)'),
]
